  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Northern Mozambique Protracted Displacement: Facilitating Durable Solutions through an Evidence-Based Approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ongoing crisis in Mozambique, triggered by natural hazards and attacks by non-state armed groups, continues to exacerbate pre-existing vulnerabilities, protracted displacement, and multi-sectoral needs. The combined impacts of interruption to essential services, unpredictable security conditions and multiple hazardous events in the country have led to increasingly complex and mixed-mobility flows. Between 2017 and 2023, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) has observed the evolution of new, renewed and protracted displacements faced by 834,000 internally displaced persons, as well as the presence of 420,000 returnees in newly accessible locations across Northern Mozambique, as of April 2023. The majority of those displaced are children and children, with a significant number of individuals with disabilities. This underscores the imperative need to ensure evidence-based interventions that address the specific needs and conditions experienced by vulnerable populations. 
 
To strengthen evidence-based interventions for integrated resilience, social cohesion and solutions for communities impacted by protracted displacement in northern Mozambique, IOM is conducting area-based solutions assessments and analysis across select districts to collect primary data on livelihoods, access to services, stability and social cohesion.</narrative>
  </description>
